# .gitleaksignore — reviewed false positives, pinned to an exact finding.
#
# CONVENTION. Each entry is a gitleaks fingerprint: <commit>:<file>:<rule>:<line>.
# That is the narrowest dismissal the tool offers — it silences ONE finding at ONE
# line of ONE file in ONE commit, under ONE rule. Every other line, file, commit and
# rule stays fully scanned. Prefer this over a .gitleaks.toml allowlist, which
# exempts a path or pattern wholesale; that file deliberately holds a single entry
# (a fixture of intentionally-fake tokens) and should stay that way.
#
# An entry belongs here ONLY when the finding is verified not to be a secret AND the
# pattern cannot be removed from history. When a pattern can be eliminated at the
# source, eliminate it — an ignore is the fallback, not the first move.

# The Check 63 registration entry was written "release-version-tokens:_c63_compute_verdict"
# — a check id and a shell function name. Gitleaks' generic-api-key rule reads
# <identifier>:<value> as a credential pair. There is no secret: both halves are
# source identifiers, and the function is defined in the same file.
#
# The pattern was ELIMINATED at the source in abf62790 (separator changed to "|" in
# both registration entries and both parse expressions), so it cannot recur as new
# checks are registered. This entry exists only because gitleaks scans full history
# (security.yml uses fetch-depth: 0 with `gitleaks detect --source .`), so the
# introducing commit keeps matching forever. Rewriting history was rejected: the
# release forbids force-push, and eight sub-task records cite commit SHAs on this
# branch.
